# TODO - Shared dependency selection

## Objective

Evaluate reusable Rust libraries before creating new mechanisms.

## Acceptance

- Evaluate storytold/craft-libs and other necessary ecosystem crates.
- Verify licensing, provenance, portability, performance and maintenance.
- Centralise adopted versions and update third-party notices.

## Evidence

Inspected Craft Libraries snapshot
`a9949872bd91ecf6bcec4e86339e3f50ad007fb9`, its NOTICE and ZIP interfaces.
The upstream offers MIT or Apache-2.0. Its archive convenience layer
loads decoded entries into memory and does not expose Unix file modes.
The newer upstream ZIP backend provides header
consistency checks. The indexed archive API detects
duplicates, overlap and mismatched headers. The iterable reader is exposed
through an explicitly unstable API; the workspace now pins ZIP exactly to
9.8.2 to use it for incremental metadata and raw entry-count admission.
The indexed archive retains the global consistency checks. ZIP 9.8.2
is adopted with only stored and pure-Rust Deflate support. No Craft source
was incorporated. Native staging uses Nix
instead of Rustix and tempfile to avoid duplicate dependency versions.
The asset contract reuses the existing SHA-2 and Serde ecosystem.
Serde 1.0.228 and the wasm-bindgen 0.2.127 family are retained to use one
compatible syn major version with the ZIP backend and TLS dependencies.
The pins must be revisited as upstream dependencies converge.
The later current-scope review below supersedes that initial audit limit.

Native graphics evaluation found no suitable Craft Libraries window or
GPU presentation backend in the inspected snapshot. Rust-SDL2 0.39.0
(MIT; its sys crate MIT and Zlib) provides a safe interface to accelerated
2D textures, scaling and native events. Adopted with pkg-config dynamic
linking to the installed Linux SDL2 2.32.74 interface; no bundled C source
or optional image, audio or font libraries. The lockfile adds no duplicate
dependency versions. A software read-back and an opt-in accelerated
window smoke test passed in run 1795582311633882091-3468901.
The interactive feature of the unified platform crate keeps this native
dependency out of independent headless engine and importer graphs. Native
Android, browser and distribution acceptance remain open; later performance
evidence is recorded below.

The inspected Craft Libraries audio crate exposes WAV PCM extraction,
but that operation intentionally accepts truncated payloads and its
decode-native feature enables unrelated codecs. The reference capture
reader needs exact RIFF and data extents and borrowed signed PCM16 bytes.
A narrow allocation-free canonical-header reader therefore admits only
the observed 44-byte RIFF PCM16 layout, not general WAV files. No new
dependency or codec implementation was added.

Current-scope dependency review, 2026-11-25: `cargo download craft-libs`
resolved the same upstream revision, and `cargo download dependencies`
materialised all 107 exact registry package versions in the locked
all-feature metadata. Each downloaded registry package includes its VCS
source receipt and declares a repository and licence. The 104 distinct
package names and their licence expressions exactly match
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. This inventory covers declarations, not legal
clearance. The native package command separately follows the selected
normal and build dependency graph, includes required licence texts and
rejects unreviewed licence expressions. Native and release-checks own
system library portability and final distribution acceptance.

Further Craft review: craft-window supplies desktop decorations and
window lists, craft-ui/menu target egui controls, and craft-platform owns
application lifecycle services rather than a game texture renderer or
controller event pump. Craft-keymap models desktop command chords,
whereas the existing game input router preserves independently held
physical sources and timed controller, touch and keyboard transitions.
Craft-atomic resolves destination symlinks, while the shared game store
requires descriptor-relative, no-follow admission and an explicit
post-rename durability outcome. Craft-settings builds on that different
storage policy. None replaces an existing game mechanism without losing
required semantics or adding an unrelated UI/backend dependency.

Direct dependency versions, optional features and native target tables
remain centralised in the root and platform manifests respectively. The
registry sources include recent maintenance evidence: ZIP 9.8.2
(2026-11-23) fixes end-record validation; Nix 0.31.3 (2026-05-11) adds
and corrects platform support; SHA-2 0.11.1 (2026-10-12) corrects ARM64
round-constant loading; zeroize 1.9.2 (2026-11-17) optimises clearing and
updates its fallback barrier. Rust-SDL2 0.39.0's changelog documents
soundness and native compatibility fixes. Rustls uses the explicit Ring
backend, Rcgen is test-only, and SHA-2, Serde, JSON, TOML and proc-macro2
serve the existing integrity, manifest and validation contracts. No new
dependency, code copy or source override is proposed.

The current validator checks six portable core targets, independent
all-feature graphs, native dependency placement and source-decoder
exclusion from runtimes. The completed performance record demonstrates
actual accelerated SDL presentation on the RTX 4060, authenticated
loopback and bounded native audio; it also records measured graphics
import times, including ZIP decoding, and exact output identities.
These are integrated workload observations, not isolated comparative
library benchmarks or universal performance guarantees. Full native
backend, physical-device and distribution acceptance stay in their
existing records. The exact ZIP pin and current transitive version
constraints remain upgrade considerations rather than completed audits
of future upstream revisions.

Acceptance recheck, 2026-11-25: both download commands succeeded again.
The current Cargo.lock contains 107 registry package entries; all 107
VCS receipts exist in the local source inventory. Locked all-feature
metadata has no missing repository or licence declaration and its 104
unique name/licence pairs exactly match the tracked notice table.
The upstream revision is unchanged. The P1 acceptance gate and signed
hook passed fresh validation 1795664853443815169-476326 and
1795664875242096638-481939 with all six portable-core targets. The
current-scope selection is accepted; final distribution, future
versions and workload-specific native measurements remain separate work.
